# THE GOLDENS ARE COMPARED BYTE FOR BYTE, so they must reach disk as they are
# stored. core.autocrlf is true here, and it was rewriting all four of these on
# every checkout: the blob is LF and the working file came back CRLF, so the
# bytes the tests compared were not the bytes git holds. expected-cases.tsv
# failed loudly -- its reader splits on newline and the blank line came back as
# a lone CR, which has no tab, so JSON.parse got EOF and the whole suite loaded
# 0 tests -- and expected-laws.txt would have failed a byte-for-byte compare
# quietly. `arest` was already pinned this way; the rest belong with it.
# There is no expected-laws.txt: the law report's own verdict is the gate,
# since a copy of its text falls behind every law added.
engine/shared/expected-cases.tsv -text
engine/shared/expected-root.txt -text
engine/shared/scenarios.canon -text

